  "summary": "Black dots of pure power: the visual sound of cosmic energy in comics.",
  "description": "The Kirby Krackle — clusters of black dots surrounding energy bursts, cosmic entities and dimensional rifts — is Jack Kirby's signature invention, standard from his 1960s-70s Marvel work (Fantastic Four, Fourth World, Eternals). Where other artists used white voids, Kirby packed negative space with crackling dot-masses that read as audible hum and raw force; combined with collage skies, square-fingered gestalt and out-of-panel bursts, the Krackle made machinery of magic. The device is now an industry-wide convention — named, taught in art schools, quoted in Manga (Bleach's release energy) and pixel games — and remains the fastest way to draw \"power\" in ink.",
  "culturalContext": "Kirby's energy dots became comics' universal power language: named, taught, quoted across manga and games, the Krackle is the fastest way to draw force — and the Kirby Museum's scholarship treats it as the signature invention of comics' most influential artist.",
